diff --git a/README.extra.md b/README.extra.md
index 6f7a9bf..cfd3e73 100644
--- a/README.extra.md
+++ b/README.extra.md
@@ -467,3 +467,53 @@ avbroot zip info -i <input OTA>
 This subcommand shows all of the OTA metadata fields. If both the modern protobuf metadata and the legacy plain text metadata exist, the protobuf metadata takes precedence.
 
 (All of the `zip` subcommands show this information. This specific subcommand just does so without performing any other operation.)
+
+## External filesystem/runtime care-map proof (integration candidate)
+
+`avbroot ota patch --expected-care-map expected.pb` installs a protobuf care map
+provided by a caller that independently derives fingerprints from authenticated
+filesystem properties. `avbroot ota verify --expected-care-map expected.pb`
+requires exact normalized equality with the OTA map. These options replace only
+the legacy AVB-property fingerprint equality rule in verification; every OTA
+signature, payload signature, partition SHA-256 and applicable AVB verification
+check remains active. Patching retains its existing signing behavior and checks
+map safety against final payload operations; callers must verify the authenticated
+source before patching and verify the final signed result afterward. An expected
+map never turns a failed authentication check into success.
+
+The external map is a **trust input**, not proof of filesystem properties by
+itself. The helper must authenticate the source, re-extract actual output images,
+bind their hashes and sizes to the same successful verification invocation, and
+read the canonical runtime property from each filesystem. It must reject missing
+or mismatching properties. Calling `--expected-care-map` with an arbitrary map
+alone does not establish the runtime fingerprint identity.
+
+The engine requires every dynamic partition, unique names, exact
+`ro.<partition>.build.fingerprint` IDs, nonempty fingerprints and canonical,
+nonempty half-open ranges within AVB-protected data. Android uses 4096-byte
+care-map blocks; this interface currently requires 4096-byte payload blocks.
+Every cared block must be written by REPLACE, REPLACE_BZ, REPLACE_XZ or ZERO.
+Overlapping writes to cared blocks are rejected to avoid relying on operation
+execution order. DISCARD is permitted outside care ranges, but never inside them, even if an
+additional operation also writes the block. New allocations are supported when
+final payload writes prove coverage; source-range inclusion is not required.
+Expected-map patching fully recompresses modified images, including the implicit
+system OTA-certificate patch, rather than reusing sparse source operations.
+
+`avbroot ota verify --verification-report report.json` writes the schema
+`avbroot-ota-verification-v1` only after successful verification. The report
+contains all partition names, authenticated image SHA-256 and byte sizes, exact
+AVB hashtree protected byte sizes for required care-map partitions, and the list
+of required care-map partitions. `avb_headers` records names and flags of the
+verified vbmeta root and chained headers; final-output consumers must enforce
+their intended AVB policy, whereas authentic sources may carry disabled flags.
+The output must not already exist and is
+installed atomically. It is a local receipt, not a signed portable attestation;
+callers must keep it bound to the invocation and archive bytes they verified.
+Without `--expected-care-map`, existing AVB-derived care-map equality remains
+unchanged. Neither interface promotes production tool pins or publication policy.
+
+This candidate also closes an existing signer-trust gap: when a public key or
+key digest is explicitly required, an unsigned vbmeta header now fails instead
+of bypassing that trust comparison. Verification without a trust key retains
+the existing ability to inspect unsigned images.
